-/*
- * MGS2 program binary cache.
- *
- * Measured on this device: compiling a GLSL shader costs 2-4 ms, but linking one
- * program on the Mali blob costs about 195 ms, and it happens synchronously in
- * whatever frame first needs that state combination. That is exactly the freeze
- * the owner reports on entering an open area or a cutscene -- a 236 ms frame was
- * caught in the same second as a 198 ms link.
- *
- * GLES gives the fix: keep the linked program's binary and hand it back with
- * glProgramBinary next time instead of linking again. The link disappears; the
- * cheap compile stays. Nothing is cached in the first session that produced it,
- * so the win shows from the second run of a scene onwards.
- *
- * The key is a hash over every attached shader's final source plus the driver
- * identity, so a driver update or a wined3d change invalidates entries instead of
- * feeding a stale binary to the GPU. glProgramBinary is always checked with
- * GL_LINK_STATUS, and any failure falls back to a normal link -- the spec
- * explicitly requires that, binaries are allowed to be rejected at any time.
- *
- *   MGS2_GLSL_CACHE=<dos path>   enable, e.g. D:\\roms\\ports\\MGS2-Substance\\cache
- *   MGS2_GLSL_CACHE_STATS=1      one line per hit/miss decision
- */
